<h1 id=3D"graphs">Graphs</h1>
<div class=3D"admonition danger">
<p class=3D"admonition-title">Don't use a nail gun unless you need a nail g=
un</p>
<p>If PydanticAI <a data-preview=3D"" href=3D"https://ai.pydantic.dev/agent=
s/">agents</a> are a hammer, and <a data-preview=3D"" href=3D"https://ai.py=
dantic.dev/multi-agent-applications/">multi-agent workflows</a> are a sledg=
ehammer, then graphs are a nail gun:</p>
<ul>
<li>sure, nail guns look cooler than hammers</li>
<li>but nail guns take a lot more setup than hammers</li>
<li>and nail guns don't make you a better builder, they make you a builder =
with a nail gun</li>
<li>Lastly, (and at the risk of torturing this metaphor), if you're a fan o=
f medieval tools like mallets and untyped Python, you probably won't like n=
ail guns or our approach to graphs. (But then again, if you're not a fan of=
 type hints in Python, you've probably already bounced off PydanticAI to us=
e one of the toy agent frameworks =E2=80=94 good luck, and feel free to bor=
row my sledgehammer when you realize you need it)</li>
</ul>
<p>In short, graphs are a powerful tool, but they're not the right tool for=
 every job. Please consider other <a data-preview=3D"" href=3D"https://ai.p=
ydantic.dev/multi-agent-applications/">multi-agent approaches</a> before pr=
oceeding.</p>
<p>If you're not confident a graph-based approach is a good idea, it might =
be unnecessary.</p>
</div>
<p>Graphs and finite state machines (FSMs) are a powerful abstraction to mo=
del, execute, control and visualize complex workflows.</p>
<p>Alongside PydanticAI, we've developed <code>pydantic-graph</code> =E2=80=
=94 an async graph and state machine library for Python where nodes and edg=
es are defined using type hints.</p>
<p>While this library is developed as part of PydanticAI; it has no depende=
ncy on <code>pydantic-ai</code> and can be considered as a pure  graph-base=
d state machine library. You may find it useful whether or not you're using=
 PydanticAI or even building with GenAI.</p>
<p><code>pydantic-graph</code> is designed for advanced users and makes hea=
vy use of Python generics and types hints. It is not designed to be as begi=
nner-friendly as PydanticAI.</p>
<div class=3D"admonition note">
<p class=3D"admonition-title">Very Early beta</p>
<p>Graph support was <a href=3D"https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic-ai/pul=
l/528">introduced</a> in v0.0.19 and is in very earlier beta. The API is su=
bject to change. The documentation is incomplete. The implementation is inc=
omplete.</p>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"installation">Installation</h2>
<p><code>pydantic-graph</code> is a required dependency of <code>pydantic-a=
i</code>, and an optional dependency of <code>pydantic-ai-slim</code>, see =
<a data-preview=3D"" href=3D"https://ai.pydantic.dev/install/#slim-install"=
>installation instructions</a> for more information. You can also install i=
t directly:</p>
<div class=3D"tabbed-set tabbed-alternate" data-tabs=3D"1:2" style=3D"--md-=
indicator-x: 0px; --md-indicator-width: 50px;"><input checked=3D"checked" i=
d=3D"__tabbed_1_1" name=3D"__tabbed_1" type=3D"radio"><input id=3D"__tabbed=
